            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Sappho's Life</strong><br>\nSappho was born around 630 BCE on the island of Lesbos, Greece. Little is known about her life, but she was recognized in her time as a great poet. Her work was highly regarded in ancient Greece, and she was often referred to as the \"Tenth Muse.\" Sappho's poetry mainly focused on personal feelings and relationships, which set her apart from her contemporaries who wrote about the gods and heroes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Her Work and Poetry</strong><br>\nSappho's poetry was composed for performance with lyre accompaniment. Unfortunately, most of her poetry has been lost; only one complete poem and fragments of others survive today. Her work is distinctive for its emotional depth and focus on the beauty and complexities of love, which was a departure from the epic themes popular in the work of her male peers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Writing Style</strong><br>\nSappho is famous for using the lyrical meter known as \"Sapphic meter,\" which has a specific rhythmic pattern. Her poems were personal, passionate, and intimate, often addressing the subjects of love and longing. Despite the scanty survival of her work, these characteristics have influenced poets for centuries.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
