            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Family and Followers</strong><br>\nThere is much speculation about Sappho's family life, with some sources suggesting she had a daughter named Cleis. Sappho was a central figure in a community of young women and girls who were her followers or students, possibly being trained by her in music and poetry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Island of Lesbos</strong><br>\nLesbos, where Sappho lived, was a cultural center in the Aegean Sea with a thriving arts scene. It was known for its school of poetry and its high level of literacy among both men and women. Sappho's work reflects the environment in which she was writing, with references to the natural beauty of the island and its social events.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reputation and Influence</strong><br>\nSappho's reputation was that of a leading poet of her time. She influenced the lyrical traditions of both ancient and modern times. Her contemporary Alcaeus is said to have described her work as \"violet-haired, pure, honey-smiling Sappho.\" Centuries later, her fragments would captivate translators and readers alike.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
